@* Switch's Overview, written by hand after Button's, Card's and MultiSelect's: settings rows (at
   the end, with icons), saving as it changes on an interactive page, and states. Accessibility
   and See also come from the doc comment; See also adds the notification settings demo. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A switch is a <Anchor Href="docs/components/checkbox">Checkbox</Anchor> that reads as on or off. It suits settings. It posts the same way, <Code>true</Code> or <Code>false</Code>, so it works on static pages with <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">JavaScript off</Anchor>. For agreeing to something or choosing an item, use a checkbox.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">At the end of the row</Heading>
<TextBlock>In a list of settings, the switch usually sits at the end of the row, with the hint under the label. To put it there, set <Code>ControlPosition.End</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="switch-control-end" />

<Heading Level="2">Icons on rows</Heading>
<TextBlock>Each switch's row can show an icon at its start. Set <Code>Icon</Code> to add one. To group the rows under a heading, put them in a <Anchor Href="docs/components/fieldset">Fieldset</Anchor> with <Code>Dividers</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="switch-icons" />
<TextBlock>The icon is decorative, so give every row in a group an icon, or none.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Saving as it changes</Heading>
<TextBlock>On interactive pages, a switch can save each change as it happens. Bind it with <Code>@@bind-Value:after</Code> and save in the method it calls. Static pages save when the form posts, so they need a Save button.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="switch-save-on-change" />
<TextBlock>To hide the Save button on interactive pages, check <Code>AssignedRenderMode</Code> as well as <Code>RendererInfo.IsInteractive</Code>. <Code>RendererInfo.IsInteractive</Code> is false while the page prerenders, so checking it alone shows the button and then hides it.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>To line the switch up with buttons and other fields, set <Code>Size</Code>. For a switch the user can't change, set <Code>Disabled</Code>; a disabled switch doesn't post.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>There's no <Code>ReadOnly</Code>, because HTML has no read-only checkbox. In a read-only <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>, the switch is disabled, and a hidden field posts its value.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="switch-states" />
<TextBlock>Before saving a disabled value from a form post, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#disabled-fields-and-form-posts">Disabled fields and form posts</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        @* The label comes from the property's [Display(Name)], or Label. *@
        <Switch @bind-Value="Model!.EmailNotifications" />

        @* A settings row: the switch at the end, the hint under the label. *@
        <Switch @bind-Value="Model!.AutoDeploy" Label="Automatic deploys"
                ControlPosition="ControlPosition.End" Hint="Deploy main when its checks pass." />
        """;
}
